ere a winning fork or immediate win? No.\n\nAnother idea: B1 or B3? Vertical in B.\n\nIf I take B1, then B1–B2–B3 is a threat. But B3 is free. Then next move I could win via B3 if not blocked.\n\nSame power as row 2 play.\n\nBut row 2 is better because it also interferes with X\'s column C.\n\nMoreover, C2 directly pressures that area.\n\nBut let’s compare:\n\nMove C2:\n- Creates immediate two-in-row (row 2)\n- Threatens win at A2\n- Blocks X’s column C progression (since C2 taken by O)\n- Central and active\n\nMove B1:\n- Creates vertical two-in-column (B1–B2), threatens B3\n- Less disruptive to X\n\nSo C2 is better due to dual purpose.\n\nBut wait — what if X plays A2 after I play C2? Then row 2 is O–O–O? No: A2 would be X, so row 2: X | O | O — not a win for X.\n\nNo, X can’t win on row 2.\n\nSo if I play C2, X cannot win by taking A2 — they just block my win.\n\nSo yes, after C2, my next move A2 would win — unless blocked.\n\nSo X must respond to A2 or risk losing.\n\nTherefore, C2 is a strong move.\n\nAlternatively, if I play A2 now, same thing: row 2 becomes A2=O, B2=O, C2=free — then I threaten C2 next.\n\nSame threat level.\n\nSo A2 and C2 are symmetric in value.\n\nBut: C2 is adjacent to X’s piece at C3. So if I play C2, I also prevent X from easily expanding in column C.\n\nMoreover, if I don’t take C2, X might take it next to build toward C1–C2–C3.\n\nSo C2 is slightly more critical defensively.\n\nTherefore, C2 is the best move.\n\nReason: Playing C2 creates an immediate two-in-a-row in row 2 (B2–C2), threatening to win on the next move by taking A2. It also blocks X from easily pursuing a column C win (C1–C2–C3). This move maximizes offensive pressure and defensive safety.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "C", "row": 2}'}
